[[File:Billy Ray Cyrus 2019.jpg|thumb|300x300px|Assisted by a remix featuring [[Billy Ray Cyrus]] (pictured), "Old Town Road" charted at number one on the ''Billboard'' Hot 100 for a record-setting nineteen weeks.]]
In the United States, the original version of "Old Town Road" reached number one on the ''Billboard'' Hot 100 in the week ending April 13, 2019;<ref name="Hot 100"/> at one minute and 53 seconds in length, it became the shortest number-one single since "[[I'm Henry VIII, I Am]]" by [[Herman's Hermits]] in 1965 and the fifth-shortest in the history of the chart.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.stereogum.com/2039096/lil-nas-xs-old-town-road-is-the-second-shortest-1-single-of-all-time/news/|title=Lil Nas X's "Old Town Road" Is The Shortest #1 Single Since 1965|date=April 9, 2019|website=Stereogum|access-date=April 17, 2019|archive-date=April 12, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190412221741/https://www.stereogum.com/2039096/lil-nas-xs-old-town-road-is-the-second-shortest-1-single-of-all-time/news/|url-status=live}}</ref> In the following week, it and the newly released remix featuring Billy Ray Cyrus received a combined 143 million streams, surpassing [[Drake (musician)|Drake]]'s "[[In My Feelings]]", which in the previous year had amassed 116.2 million in the same period, as the song with the highest single-week streaming figure in the country.<ref name="BillboardApr20">{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/old-town-road-lil-nas-x-billy-ray-cyrus-hot-100-number-one/|title=Lil Nas X's 'Old Town Road,' Featuring Billy Ray Cyrus, Spends Second Week at No. 1 on ''Billboard'' Hot 100 with Record-Shattering Streams|magazine=Billboard|url-access=subscription}}</ref> This song also became Cyrus's biggest hit in the U.S. since "[[Achy Breaky Heart]]" peaked at number four on the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] 27 years prior.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/2019-08-17|title=Top 100 Songs {{!}} Billboard Hot 100 Chart|magazine=Billboard|access-date=August 16, 2019}}</ref> "Old Town Road" continued to exceed 100 million streams in each of the eight following weeks, and in June 2019, was responsible for the three highest weekly U.S. streaming totals in the history of digital music, as well as nine of the eleven highest overall.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Trust |first=Gary |title=Lil Nas X's 'Old Town Road' Tops ''Billboard'' Hot 100 for Ninth Week, the Most This Year, & DaBaby's 'Suge' Hits Top 10 |url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/lil-nas-x-old-town-road-hot-100-number-one-ninth-week/ |magazine=Billboard |access-date=June 3, 2019 |url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref name="BillboardAug17"/> In October 2019, "Old Town Road" became the fastest single in RIAA history to receive diamond certification in the United States. The certification, which is awarded to singles that attain national combined sales and streams of ten million units, was achieved by the song in its eleventh month of release.<ref name=RIAA /><ref>{{cite web |last1=Wheat |first1=Lorraine |title=Lil Nas X's 'Old Town Road' Is Certified Diamond |date=October 23, 2019 |url=https://variety.com/2019/music/news/lil-nas-x-old-town-road-billy-ray-cyrus-certified-diamond-1203380313/ |website=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]  |access-date=November 11, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191023184350/https://variety.com/2019/music/news/lil-nas-x-old-town-road-billy-ray-cyrus-certified-diamond-1203380313/ |archive-date=October 23, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> The single had sold 1.6 million digital copies in the United States by February 2020.<ref>{{cite web |last=Bjorke |first=Matt |title=Top 30 Digital Country Singles Sales Chart: March 2, 2020 |url=http://roughstock.com/news/2020/03/44155-top-30-digital-country-singles-sales-chart-march-2-2020 |work=Rough Stock |date=March 4, 2020 |access-date=March 14, 2020 |archive-date=April 4, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210404163252/http://roughstock.com/news/2020/03/44155-top-30-digital-country-singles-sales-chart-march-2-2020 |url-status=live }}</ref>

"Old Town Road" topped the Hot 100 for 19 consecutive weeks from April 13 to August 17, 2019. On August 24, it was finally unseated by [[Billie Eilish]]'s "[[Bad Guy (Billie Eilish song)|Bad Guy]]" which had waited in the runner-up spot for nine nonconsecutive weeks. "Old Town Road" holds the record for the most consecutive weeks at number one on the Hot 100 chart, surpassing the previous record of 16 weeks achieved by both "[[One Sweet Day]]" by [[Mariah Carey]] and [[Boyz II Men]] (1995–96), and "[[Despacito#Justin Bieber remix version|Despacito]]" by [[Luis Fonsi]] and [[Daddy Yankee]] featuring [[Justin Bieber]] (2017).<ref name="BillboardAug17">{{cite magazine |last1=Trust |first1=Gary |date=August 12, 2019 |title=Lil Nas X's 'Old Town Road' Leads ''Billboard'' Hot 100 for 19th Week, Ariana Grande & Social House's 'Boyfriend' Debuts in Top 10 |url=https://www.billboard.com/articles/business/chart-beat/8527171/lil-nas-x-old-town-road-number-one-hot-100-19-weeks |magazine=Billboard |access-date=August 12, 2019 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190812191901/https://www.billboard.com/articles/business/chart-beat/8527171/lil-nas-x-old-town-road-number-one-hot-100-19-weeks |archive-date=August 12, 2019}}</ref> Mariah Carey's "[[All I Want for Christmas Is You]]" broke its record for longest-running number one song (which it held with [[Shaboozey]]'s "[[A Bar Song (Tipsy)]]", which tied its record with 19 non-consecutive weeks at number one in November 2024)<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-12-13 |title=How Shaboozey's A Bar Song (Tipsy) became the unexpected smash hit of 2024 |url=https://www.bbc.com/culture/article/20241212-shaboozey-a-bar-song-tipsy-the-2024-drinking-song-that-became-a-giant-us-hit |access-date=2025-06-13 |website=BBC |language=en-GB}}</ref> in December 2025, achieving 20 non-consecutive weeks at number one on the chart dated December 20, 2025 ("Christmas" has since achieved 22 non-consecutive weeks at number one as of the chart dated January 3, 2026).<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/lists/mariah-carey-hot-100-christmas-19-weeks/|title=Mariah Carey's 'All I Want for Christmas Is You' Notches Record-Tying 19th Week at No. 1 on Billboard Hot 100|last=Trust|first=Gary|magazine=Billboard|date=December 8, 2025|access-date=December 8, 2025}}</ref> In July 2019, "Old Town Road" also became the first number-one single on the [[Rolling Stone Top 100|''Rolling Stone'' Top 100]] chart when it held the position in the chart's inaugural edition.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/rs-charts-top-100-lil-nas-x-shawn-mendes-drake-847955/|title=RS Charts: Lil Nas X's 'Old Town Road' Is Number One on Rolling Stone Top 100 Chart|date=July 2, 2019|access-date=July 3, 2019|magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]}}</ref> In accordance with their methodologies, ''Billboard'' and ''Rolling Stone'' each assign a single position to "Old Town Road" on their respective charts by combining the performance figures of every released version of the song determined to be sufficiently similar to the original.<ref name="Abshire">{{cite news |last1=Abshire |first1=Emily |title=The Remix Race: Why It Pays to Release Songs More Than Once |url=https://www.npr.org/2019/07/18/743121439/lil-nas-x-old-town-road-billie-eilish-bad-guy-remix-billboard-charts |website=NPR |date=July 18, 2019 |access-date=July 31, 2019 |archive-date=October 4, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191004143614/https://www.npr.org/2019/07/18/743121439/lil-nas-x-old-town-road-billie-eilish-bad-guy-remix-billboard-charts |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |title=General Methodology of the ''Rolling Stone'' Charts |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/charts-methodology/ |magazine=Rolling Stone  |date=July 2, 2019 |access-date=November 13, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190702224502/https://www.rollingstone.com/charts-methodology/ |archive-date=July 2, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> The former publication credits the entirety of the song's chart run solely to the remix featuring Cyrus.<ref name="BillboardApr20" /><ref name="Abshire"/>

Elsewhere, the original version of the song topped the national singles charts in at least ten other countries — Australia (thirteen weeks),<ref>{{cite web |title=13 Weeks at #1 for Lil Nas X's Old Town Road |url=https://www.ariacharts.com.au/news/2019/13-weeks-at-1-for-lil-nas-x-s-old-town-road |website=[[ARIA Charts]] |publisher=[[Australian Recording Industry Association]] |access-date=August 15, 2019 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190723210516/https://www.ariacharts.com.au/news/2019/13-weeks-at-1-for-lil-nas-x-s-old-town-road |archive-date=July 23, 2019}}</ref> France and Norway (eight weeks each),<ref>* {{cite web |title=Top singles (Téléchargement + streaming): 2019 – Semaine du 31 mai |trans-title=Top Singles (Download + Streaming): 2019 – Week of 31 May |url=http://www.snepmusique.com/tops-semaine/top-singles-megafusion/?ye=2019&we=22 |publisher=[[Syndicat National de l'Édition Phonographique]] |access-date=August 16, 2019 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190816011457/http://www.snepmusique.com/tops-semaine/top-singles-megafusion/?ye=2019&we=22 |archive-date=August 16, 2019 |language=fr }}
